57ef4904-5bc4-59c1-981b-64581ac6a7dc	READING_COMPREHENSION	Why does the author mention that Kalgoorlie stands about 400 metres higher than the dam?	Water Uphill to Kalgoorlie\nIn 1892, prospectors found gold at Coolgardie, in the dry country more than five hundred kilometres east of Perth. A year later, an Irish prospector named Paddy Hannan made another find nearby, at what became Kalgoorlie. Tens of thousands of people rushed to the area hoping to make their fortunes. They found gold, but they also found one of the harshest environments in Australia. Rain was scarce and unreliable, there were no permanent rivers, and the little groundwater available was often too salty to drink.\nWater quickly became more precious than the gold itself. It was carted in on camels, collected in tanks, or produced by condensers that boiled salty water and captured the steam. In hot summers, a bucket of water could cost more than a meal. Without clean water, diseases such as typhoid spread through the crowded camps, and many people died.\nThe government of Western Australia turned to its Engineer-in-Chief, Charles Yelverton O'Connor, who had already built the harbour at Fremantle. His plan was astonishing for its time. He proposed building a dam on the Helena River in the hills near Perth and pumping water through a pipeline about 530 kilometres long to the goldfields. The difficulty was not only the distance. Kalgoorlie stands about 400 metres higher than the dam, so the water would have to be pushed uphill most of the way.\nMany people thought the idea was impossible or wasteful. Newspapers mocked it, and some politicians argued that the gold might run out before the pipeline was finished, leaving the colony with an enormous debt and a pipe to nowhere. O'Connor and the Premier, John Forrest, held firm, and work began in 1898.\nThe project used new technology. Instead of riveting the steel pipes, engineers used a locking-bar method that clamped the edges of each pipe together, making