8da935f3-4baf-5364-bcca-01247cf91171	READING_COMPREHENSION	Why is the background painted on a curved wall?	Windows into the Wild\nWalk into many natural history museums and you will find glass cases that seem to open onto another world: a misty rainforest, a snowy mountain slope or a billabong at sunset. These scenes are called dioramas. Although each one looks like a window, it is really a clever illusion built by a team of artists and scientists.\nCreating a diorama usually happens in several stages.\nFirst, the team studies the real place. Artists visit the location, take photographs, make sketches and collect samples of soil, leaves and rocks. Scientists check which animals and plants really belong together, and at what time of year.\nNext, a painter creates the background on a curved wall at the back of the case. Because the wall curves, there are no corners to give the trick away. Distant hills and sky are painted in soft, pale colours, since faraway things look hazy to our eyes.\nThen the foreground is built. This is the part closest to the glass. It holds real or carefully copied rocks, logs and soil, along with model leaves made one by one from wax, paper or plastic. Some dioramas contain thousands of handmade leaves.\nThe hardest job is joining the two parts. Where the real ground meets the painted wall, the colours and shapes must match exactly. If they do, visitors cannot tell where the solid ground ends and the painting begins.\nFinally, preserved animals or lifelike models are put in place, and the lighting is set. Lights hidden above the case can make a scene look like early morning or late afternoon.\nSome visitors today prefer screens and touch displays, and a few museums have removed their older dioramas. Yet many people still stand at the glass for minutes at a time. A good diorama lets you see an animal in its home, all in a single glance, in a way that a label or a photograph never could.	\N	C	\N	\N	["NAPLAN", "Year 5", "Mock 1"]	PUBLISHED	f34c5