44a542b8-913d-5fff-982e-fad477435e0b	READING_COMPREHENSION	Which words best describe the tone of the text?	On the Tip of My Tongue\nA quiz kid called Lachlan McRae\nKnew the answer but just couldn't say.\nIt was there on his tongue,\nLike a bell left unrung,\nAnd it came to him later that day.\nYou know the feeling. You are trying to remember the name of a film, a song or the kid who sat next to you in Year 2. You might even know that it starts with 'M' or has three syllables, but the word itself refuses to come out. Scientists have a name for this everyday mishap. They call it a tip-of-the-tongue moment.\nIn 1966, two researchers at Harvard University decided to study it. They read out dictionary definitions of rare words, such as 'sampan', and asked students to name each word. Many students got stuck partway. Often they could guess the first letter, or they came up with words that sounded similar, like 'Siam' and 'sarong'. This showed that the brain had found part of the word, but not all of it.\nSince then, studies have found that most people have a tip-of-the-tongue moment about once a week. These moments become more common as people get older, and they happen most often with names.\nSo what should you do when a word gets stuck? Strangely, trying harder can make things worse, because your brain keeps landing on the wrong word. Many experts suggest thinking about something else. The missing word often pops up on its own, sometimes hours later, usually at a moment when it is no help at all.\nMy uncle Dev learned this the hard way. At a trivia night, his team needed one last answer: the name of the longest river in Australia. Dev was sure he knew it. He scrunched up his face, drummed on the table and muttered 'Mur... Mur...' until the time ran out. His team lost by one point. Halfway home in the car, he suddenly shouted 'The Murray!' so loudly that Aunty Mel nearly dropped her phone. He still blames the trivia host.	\N	B	\N	\N	["NAPLAN", "Year 5", "Mock 1"]	PUBLISH