d9a1e037-7ee4-5a03-a511-789c9458efe8	READING_COMPREHENSION	In the sentence about the children's writing, the word minute means . . .	Books the Size of a Matchbox\nIn 1826, in a stone house on the edge of the wild Yorkshire moors in England, four children received a present that would change their lives. Their father brought home a box of twelve wooden toy soldiers for Branwell, the only boy. Before long, his sisters Charlotte, Emily and Anne had each claimed a soldier of their own.\nThe Bronte children had already known sadness. Their mother had died when they were very young, and two older sisters had died too. Their aunt had moved in to help run the household, but the children spent many hours entertaining themselves. They had few friends nearby, so they turned to each other.\nThe soldiers soon came alive in their imaginations. The children gave them names, adventures and whole kingdoms to rule. Together they invented an imaginary land called Glass Town, with its own cities, newspapers and quarrels.\nThen they began writing the stories down. Because the books were meant to be read by the toy soldiers, the children made them tiny. Some were only about five centimetres tall, smaller than a matchbox. They stitched the pages together by hand and filled them with writing so minute that it is hard to read without a magnifying glass. Paper was expensive, so every scrap of space was used.\nAs they grew older, Emily and Anne broke away to create their own imaginary kingdom, called Gondal, while Charlotte and Branwell kept writing about Glass Town. Their games lasted well into adulthood.\nThose hours of play were like a training ground. All three sisters went on to become famous novelists. Charlotte wrote Jane Eyre, Emily wrote Wuthering Heights and Anne wrote Agnes Grey. Branwell became a painter.\nToday, some of the tiny books are kept in museums. Visitors peer at the handwriting and imagine four children by the fire, building whole worlds together out of a simple box of 