c21ee362-931b-5d01-8097-d09cfdbfae5e	READING_COMPREHENSION	What job did Molly's father do on the bridge?	The Gap in the Arch\nAll through the winter of 1930, Molly Fraser walked to the end of her street in North Sydney every afternoon to look at the bridge. For years it had been growing out from both sides of the harbour, two great steel arms reaching towards each other across the water. Each day the gap between them looked a little smaller.\nMolly's father was a rigger on the bridge. He left before dawn and came home with grease on his hands and stories in his head. He told her about the creeper cranes that crawled along the top of each half-arch, lifting steel into place. He told her about the riveting gangs, too: one worker heated the rivets in a little furnace until they glowed, then tossed them through the air to a catcher who snatched them in a metal cup.\n'What stops the two halves from falling into the harbour?' Molly asked one night.\n'Cables,' said Dad. 'Huge steel cables anchored in tunnels in the rock. They hold each half back, like a hand on a dog's collar.'\nIn August, the workers began to let the cables out, slowly, a little each day. The two half-arches tilted forward, inch by careful inch. Half of Sydney seemed to be watching.\nThen, late on the night of the nineteenth, the two halves touched. Molly heard the news from a neighbour running down the street, shouting. The next morning she raced to the corner. The ragged gap had gone. Instead, one enormous arch curved across the sky, steel grey against the blue, as if it had always been there.\nThat evening, Dad came home tired but smiling. Molly could hear the ships in the harbour hooting, and the clatter, clang and rattle of the riveters still working in the distance.\n'Is it finished?' she asked.\n'Not yet,' said Dad, ruffling her hair. 'But the hardest part is done. Now they can hang the road.'	\N	C	\N	\N	["NAPLAN", "Year 5", "Mock 3"]	PUBLISHED	f34c58ab-46a4-4693-8bb1-f36de4da0f46	2026-11-17 07:38: