37771023-3977-5010-9844-2f0643e873d9	READING_COMPREHENSION	Why does the writer say campers can see the stars 'well before bedtime' in winter?	Pack a Beanie: The Case for Winter Camping\nAsk most Australian families when they go camping, and they will say the summer holidays. It makes sense. The days are long, the weather is warm and school is out. But I believe that families who pack away their tents when the weather cools are missing out on the best camping of the year. It is time to give winter camping a chance.\nFirst, consider the crowds, or rather the lack of them. In January, popular campgrounds are booked out months ahead. Tents are squeezed together so tightly that you can hear your neighbours snoring. In June or July, many of those same campgrounds are almost empty. You can choose the best site, wander along quiet tracks and hear nothing at night but the wind in the trees.\nSecond, winter is often more comfortable than summer. Anyone who has tried to sleep in a tent on a 35-degree night knows how miserable it can be. In many parts of Australia, winter days are mild and sunny, perfect for long walks without the risk of heatstroke. There are fewer flies and mosquitoes to battle, too. When the temperature drops after dark, a good sleeping bag and a woolly beanie will keep you snug.\nThird, winter brings experiences that summer simply cannot offer. With fire restrictions often eased in the cooler months, many campgrounds allow campfires, where families can gather to talk, cook and toast marshmallows. Long, dark nights mean you can see the stars well before bedtime. On a clear winter night, away from city lights, the Milky Way spills across the sky like a river of sugar.\nSome people will argue that winter camping is too cold and too wet. It is true that you need to be prepared. Pack warm layers, a waterproof jacket and a tent that can handle rain, and always check the weather forecast before you leave. But a little preparation is a small price to pay. After all